     ddddbb _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddddbb utility configures certain aspects of the ddb(4) kernel debugger
     from user space that are not configured at compile-time or easily via
     sysctl(8) MIB entries.

     To ease configuration, commands can be put in a file which is processed
     using ddddbb as shown in the last synopsis line.  An absolute _p_a_t_h_n_a_m_e must
     be used.  The file will be read line by line and applied as arguments to
     the ddddbb utility.  Whitespace at the beginning of lines will be ignored as
     will lines where the first non-whitespace character is ‘#’.

OOUUTTPPUUTT CCAAPPTTUURREE
     The ddddbb utility can be used to extract the contents of the ddb(4) output
     capture buffer of the current live kernel, or from the crash dump of a
     kernel on disk.  The following debugger commands are available from the
     command line:

     ccaappttuurree [--MM _c_o_r_e] [--NN _s_y_s_t_e_m] pprriinntt
             Print the current contents of the ddb(4) output capture buffer.

     ccaappttuurree [--MM _c_o_r_e] [--NN _s_y_s_t_e_m] ssttaattuuss
             Print the current status of the ddb(4) output capture buffer.

SSCCRRIIPPTTIINNGG
     The ddddbb utility can be used to configure aspects of ddb(4) scripting from
     user space; scripting support is described in more detail in ddb(4).
     Each of the debugger commands is available from the command line:

     ssccrriipptt _s_c_r_i_p_t_n_a_m_e
             Print the script named _s_c_r_i_p_t_n_a_m_e.

     ssccrriipptt _s_c_r_i_p_t_n_a_m_e=_s_c_r_i_p_t
             Define a script named _s_c_r_i_p_t_n_a_m_e.  As many scripts contain char‐
             acters interpreted in special ways by the shell, it is advisable
             to enclose _s_c_r_i_p_t in quotes.

     ssccrriippttss
             List currently defined scripts.

     uunnssccrriipptt _s_c_r_i_p_t_n_a_m_e
             Delete the script named _s_c_r_i_p_t_n_a_m_e.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ddddbb utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following example defines a script that will execute when the kernel
     debugger is entered as a result of a break signal:

           ddb script kdb.enter.break="show pcpu; bt"

     The following example will delete the script:

           ddb unscript kdb.enter.break

     For further examples, see the ddb(4) and textdump(4) manual pages.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     ddb(4), mac_ddb(4), textdump(4), sysctl(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ddddbb utility first appeared in FreeBSD 7.1.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Robert N M Watson

BBUUGGSS
